Project: Polygon Mesh Processing Library (PMP)
URL: https://github.com/pmp-library/pmp-library
License: MIT with employer disclaimer (see LICENSE.txt)
Upstream version: 3.0.0

Only the surface mesh data structure and the algorithms that remeshing and decimation depend on
are included.
Visualization, file I/O and other algorithms are not vendored, except io/io_flags.h, which
surface_mesh.h includes.

Local modifications to src/pmp/algorithms/decimation.cpp (tagged "BLENDER MODIFICATION"):
  * Corners of features are never removed. These are vertices where other than two feature edges
    meet, or where the feature bends by 30 degrees or more.
  * The remaining vertex of a collapse moves to the position with the lowest quadric error, as in
    Garland and Heckbert, "Surface Simplification Using Quadric Error Metrics" (1997). Upstream
    only keeps original vertices, which makes convex surfaces shrink. Vertices on boundaries and
    features and unselected vertices stay in place.

Local modifications to src/pmp/algorithms/remeshing.{h,cpp} (tagged "BLENDER MODIFICATION"):
  * Added an optional progress/cancellation callback to uniform_remeshing() and
    adaptive_remeshing(). Returning false stops before the next iteration.
  * Feature vertices that bend by 30 degrees or more are locked as corners. Upstream only locks
    vertices without exactly two feature edges, so kinks along a crease were rounded away.
  * Adaptive remeshing first splits input edges longer than the maximum edge length, so large flat
    faces have vertices of their own. Otherwise they inherit the small target lengths of their
    curved borders.
  * Adaptive target lengths are limited so that they grow by at most half the distance travelled,
    which keeps the triangle size changing gradually.
  * Projection, tangential smoothing, normals and valences run on all CPU cores, using Blender's
    thread pool. Split, collapse and flip find their candidate edges in parallel and only apply
    the changes on one thread. The results do not depend on the number of threads.
  * Collapses run the expensive topological test last, and edge length tests avoid square roots.
  * Collapse does at most 3 passes per iteration instead of 10.
  * Uniform remeshing no longer projects new vertices while splitting, because all vertices are
    projected right afterwards.

Local modifications for speed (tagged "BLENDER MODIFICATION"):
  * src/pmp/algorithms/normals.cpp no longer looks up vertex positions by property name.
  * src/pmp/algorithms/decimation.cpp only looks up texture coordinates by name if they exist.
  * src/pmp/algorithms/decimation.cpp tests collapses of a vertex in order of priority and stops
    at the first legal one. This selects the same collapse as testing all of them.

The wrapper that converts to and from Blender's Mesh lives in
source/blender/geometry/intern/isotropic_remesh.cc.
